.tl ''Conditional Expressions''


The form of a conditonal expression has been extended over that of
lisp 1.5. In its most general form, it is:

	(cond
		(e1,1 e1,2 ... e1,k)
			||
		        \\/
		(en,1 ...    ...   )
	)

where n >_ 0, and k >_ 1.


The ei,1's are considered to be predicates,
and are evaluated until one is found whose value is not nil.
If this is ek,1,  then the corresponding ek,2 ... ek,n are evaluated.
The value of the conditional is ek,n. If none of the ei,1's are non-nil, then
the value of the conditional is nil. At the top level of a prog, the
cond is somewhat special.


The select______ conditional form from lisp 1.5 has also been implemented.
The general form is:

	(select q
		(q1 e1)
		  ||
		  \\/
		(qn en)
		e
	)

q_ is evaluated,  and its value is compared with the evaluated values
of the qi's until one is found which is equal_____.  The value of the selct is then the
value of the corresponding ei.  If no equality is found, the value of the
select is e_.

.bp
.tl ''Predicates''


Predicates are functions that return a value from the set
.br

.ce
{t_, nil___}

denoting truth or falsity, respectively.

The following are the basic lisp-11 predicates:

.C0
(*gt a1 a2)
.br
(*ge a1 a2)
.br
(*eq a1 a2)
.br
(*le a1 a2)
.br
(*lt a1 a2)
.br
(*ne a1 a2)
.C1
subrs
.C2
t_ is returned if a1 bears the indicated relation to a2.
These predicates are defined iff a1 and a2 are both numeric, or
both strings. For strings, the ascii collating sequence is used, and trailing
blanks are significant. 

.in 0
examples:
	(*eq 0.0 0)		yields t_.
	(*eq "cat" "dog")	yields nil___.
	(*eq "cat" 0)		is erroneous.


.C0
(eq. a1 ... an)
.br
(ge. a1 ... an)
.br
(gt. a1 ... an)
.br
(ne. a1 ... an)
.br
(lt. a1 ... an)
.br
(le. a1 ... an)
.C1
lsubrs
.C2
n_ must be >_ 1.
If n_ = 1, comparison is with 0 for numeric values, and
the null string for string values. If n > 1, these are equivalent to
(and (relx a1 a2) (relx ..) ... (relx an-1 an)),
where relx____ is the obvious relation.

.in 0
examples:
	(gt. 5 4 3 2)		yields t_
	(gt. 5 6 "dog")		yields nil___
	(gt. -1)		yields nil___


.C0
(and c1 ... cn)
.C1
nsubr
.C2
The ci's are evaluated until one is found with the value nil.___
Then nil is returned as the value of the predicate.  The remaining
ci's are not evaluated. If none of the ci's evaluate to nil, then
the value of the predicate is t_.
.in 0

example:
	(and)			yields t_


.C0
(atom s)
.C1
subr
.C2
is true if s is atomic.


.C0
(eq n m)
.C1
subr
.C2
is true if n and m have the same address. 
This will be true of interned symbolic atoms with the same print name.
Otherwise, it is usually false.


.C0
(equal n m)
.C1
subr
.C2
is true if they would have the same external representation.
(Except for non-interned symbolic atoms with the same print name -
these are not___ equal.)


.in 0
examples:
	(eq 0 0)		yields nil___
	(eq 'a 'a)		yields t
	(equal 0 0)		yields t


.C0
(fixp n)
.C1
subr
.C2
is true if n is of type int___.


.C0
(floatp n)
.C1
subr
.C2
is true if n is of type float


.C0
(member L1 l2)
.C1
subr
.C2
is true if L1 is equal_____ to a top level element of the list L2.
It is equivalent to:
.br
(lambda (L1 L2)
(cond ((atom L2) nil) ((equal L1 (car L2)) t) (t (memeber L1 (cdr L2))))
(member L1 (cdr L2))))



.C0
(memeq L1 l2)
.C1
subr
.C2
is like member______, but using the predicate eq__ rather than equal_____.


.C0
(or c1 ... cn)
.C1
nsubr
.C2
The ci's are evaluated until one is found whose value is non-nil.
Then t is returned as the value of the predicate.  The remaining ci's are not
evaluated.  If all of the ci's evaluate to nil,  then the value
of the predicate is nil.


.in 0
example:
	(or)			yields nil


.C0
(null s)
.C1
subr
.C2
is true if s is nil.


.C0
